<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Barrett Browning is a 39-year-old poet with bronchiectasis diagnosed four months ago, long-term morphine therapy that she is slowly reducing by her own choice, and prolonged grief disorder in weekly grief therapy, who comes for her yearly preventive visit. She does airway clearance twice a day, and her sputum is down to about a tablespoon a day without blood. She takes morphine solution 4 mL (8 mg) twice daily, reduced in two steps from 5 mL since April. She has been out by car twice this summer and walked in the garden. She is writing well and has finished a set of poems she began in the spring. Her periods are regular. She has no new concerns. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Periodic adult preventive visit, age 39</td></tr><tr><td>Lungs</td><td>Stable on airway clearance; no exacerbation since diagnosis</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Morphine solution 8 mg twice daily; naloxone at home; vitamin D3 1819 units daily</td></tr><tr><td>Mood</td><td>PHQ-2 1; finishing the sixteenth grief therapy session this week</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient on paper at home and reviewed with her by Dr. Haverford-Asante. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Fair, and better than in the spring</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Absorbing work and many correspondents; grief easing; financially secure</td></tr><tr><td>Substance use</td><td>AUDIT-C 0; prescribed morphine taken as directed; no other drugs</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Never uses tobacco; no smokers in her room</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Home exercise program three days a week; walks in the garden in fine weather</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Small frequent meals and one supplement drink a day; dairy daily</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>About six hours, better than in the spring</td></tr><tr><td>Safety</td><td>No falls; morphine kept locked; naloxone at home; no firearm at home; feels safe at home (intimate partner violence screen negative)</td></tr><tr><td>Sexual and reproductive health</td><td>Not sexually active; not planning pregnancy; periods regular</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

